::ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=x+2 and g(x)=3x, what is (f+g)(x)?
{
=A. 4x+2#Correct. Add the rules: x+2+3x=4x+2.
~B. 3x²+2#This multiplies instead of adding.
~C. 4x#This omits the constant 2.
~D. 3x+2#This is f(g(x)), not f+g.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=x² and g(x)=x+1, what is f(g(x))?
{
~A. x²+1#This adds 1 after squaring instead of substituting.
=B. (x+1)²#Correct. Substitute the entire expression x+1 into f.
~C. x²(x+1)#This multiplies the functions.
~D. 2x+1#This is not the composition.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=2x and g(x)=x-5, what is g(f(3))?
{
~A. -4#This applies g to 3 first only.
~B. 6#This stops after f(3).
=C. 1#Correct. First f(3)=6, then g(6)=1.
~D. 11#This adds instead of subtracting 5.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
For (f/g)(x), what values must be excluded?
{
~A. Values where f(x)=0#The numerator may be zero.
~B. All positive values#Positive values are not automatically excluded.
~C. All real values#Only denominator zeros are excluded.
=D. Values where g(x)=0#Correct. The denominator function cannot equal zero.
}

::ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=x-4 and g(x)=5x, what is f(g(x))?
{
=A. 5x-4#Correct. Substitute 5x into f.
~B. 5x(x-4)#This multiplies the functions.
~C. x-20#This substitutes incorrectly.
~D. 5x+4#The sign of the constant is wrong.
}

